<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>{{ t("you.page.you-targum", "You — targum") }}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('chrome.css') }}{{ asset('words.css') }}{{ asset('library.css') }}{{ asset('you.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
{#- The key, before anything that reads it (targum-internal#343). `building.js`, and
    the conversation's pill, the followed series and the palette in the nav, each read `window.TARGUM_KEY`
    once as they load; it was set in a script at the foot of the body, so on a local serve
    they held an empty key for the life of the page and the bell, the followed series and
    the chat list were refused. The hosted box has no key and was never affected. -#}
<script>window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";</script>
</head>
<body class="words you">
{% set here = 'you' %}{% set title = t("you.page.title", "You") %}
{% include '_nav.html.j2' %}
<script>{{ asset('building.js') }}</script>

<main>
  {#- Signed out there is nothing here to show, and saying so beats an empty form. -#}
  <section class="panel" id="stranger" hidden>
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.sign-in-from-the-corner-and-we", "Sign in from the corner and we'll fill this page in.") }}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="who" hidden>
    <div class="who-head">
      <span class="avatar-large" id="you-avatar"></span>
      <div class="who-what">
        <label class="field">
          <span>{{ t("you.page.name", "Name") }}</span>
          <input type="text" id="you-name" dir="auto" maxlength="60" autocomplete="name"
                 placeholder="{{ t("you.page.what-should-we-call-you", "What should we call you?") }}" spellcheck="false">
        </label>
        <p class="you-email" id="you-email"></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    {#- How the conversation says "you" in Hebrew (2026-09-14). Every second person and
        every present-tense "I" in Hebrew has a gender, and nikkud makes it visible; a
        conversation that guessed once rewrote a man's sentence into the feminine and
        called it a correction. -#}
    <fieldset class="address" id="you-address">
      <legend>{{ t("you.page.in-hebrew-targum-calls-you", "In Hebrew, targum calls you") }}</legend>
      <label><input type="radio" name="address" id="address-m" value="m"> <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">אַתָּה</bdi></label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="address" id="address-f" value="f"> <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">אַתְּ</bdi></label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="address" id="address-either" value=""> {{ t("you.page.either", "Either") }}</label>
    </fieldset>
    <p class="said" id="you-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  {#- Which languages you are learning, and which you read well enough to be handed a
      translation in. Every one targum has is here with its stage beside it: this is the
      page where somebody says what they read, so a language missing from it would be a
      question they had no way to answer. Drawn by `you.js` from the lists below. -#}
  <section class="panel" id="languages" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.your-languages", "Your languages") }}</h2>
    <div class="ticks">
      <span class="ticks-label" id="learning-label">{{ t("you.page.learning", "Learning") }}</span>
      <span class="tick-list" id="you-learning" role="group" aria-labelledby="learning-label"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="ticks">
      <span class="ticks-label" id="reads-label">{{ t("you.page.translations-in", "Translations in") }}</span>
      <span class="tick-list" id="you-reads" role="group" aria-labelledby="reads-label"></span>
    </div>
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.hebrew-is-always-on-everything-else-is", "Hebrew is always on. Everything else is experimental, and you lose nothing when you untick one.") }}</p>
    <p class="said" id="you-languages-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="reading" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.your-reading", "Your reading") }}</h2>
    <p class="tally" id="you-kept"></p>
    <p class="elsewhere"><a href="/progress">{{ t("you.page.your-progress", "Your Progress") }}</a> {{ t("you.page.has-the-whole-of-it", "has the whole of it.") }}</p>
  </section>

  {#- What is recorded of a sitting (targum-internal#127): on from the first session, by
      decision, and the reader's own to stop and to erase — so it is said here, plainly, with
      both controls beside it. Drawn only where this box keeps such a record at all
      (`TARGUM_EVENTS`); where it does not, there is nothing to say and nothing to switch. -#}
  <section class="panel" id="record" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.what-we-record", "What we record as you go") }}</h2>
    <p>{{ t("you.page.record-says", "When you look a word up, play a recording, turn a page or finish a section, we keep a note of it on your account. It is how Your Progress can say how long you've listened and how much you've read, and it shows us which lines are hard. We keep it for as long as you keep your account.") }}</p>
    <p class="ending-row">
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet" id="record-switch" aria-pressed="true"></button>
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet danger" id="record-erase">{{ t("you.page.record-erase", "Erase what's recorded") }}</button>
    </p>
    <p class="said" id="record-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  {#- Correcting a meaning (targum-internal#164, door 3). Behind the account rather than
      in the reader, because it is a thing you agree to once and not a thing you meet in
      the middle of a sentence. Until it is accepted the reader offers no such control at
      all — not a greyed one, which would be an invitation to a shut door. -#}
  <section class="panel" id="correcting">
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.correcting", "Correcting a meaning") }}</h2>
    <p>{{ t("you.page.correcting-says", "If a word's meaning is wrong, you can say so from the word's card and tell us what it should be. Nothing changes until a person looks at it. We keep what you send under the terms in the contribution grant, and you keep every right in it as well. Your name is never stored with it.") }}</p>
    <p class="ending-row">
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet" id="grant-go"></button>
      <a class="go-quiet" href="https://github.com/DLangellotti/targum/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#correcting-a-meaning-and-what-that-grants">{{ t("you.page.correcting-read", "Read the grant") }}</a>
    </p>
    <p class="said" id="grant-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  {#- Last, and quiet. Everything here is either slow to undo or impossible. -#}
  {#- Your subscriptions (2026-09-11): what comes out on its own clock — the weekly, the
      weekly portion, a learning cycle — each with where it is this week, one press to
      follow and one to open. Following puts the newest instalment in the sheet on Learn
      when it lands, in the bell, and in your inbox. Here, behind the account, and not on
      the Library: the main pages stay as simple as they can. Drawn by `follow.js`;
      hidden until the server has named a series. -#}
  {#- What is connected, and the way to end it (targum-internal#80). Here because this
      is where a reader comes to take something back, and because design.md §12 ("A
      scope is a press that lasts") rests on the grant being visible somewhere they can:
      a standing consent that could not be found is not one. Hidden until there is one —
      a panel saying "nothing is connected" is a panel advertising connecting, and
      /connect is where that is done. -#}
  <section class="panel" id="connections" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.what-s-connected", "What's connected") }}</h2>
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.connections-says", "Apps you've connected targum to, and what each one may do. Disconnecting takes effect at once.") }}</p>
    <ul class="series" id="connection-rows"></ul>
    <p class="said" id="connections-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  {#- What a reader wrote for their connector to offer (targum-internal#80, note 17:
      "enable users to use targum their way"). Two fields and no jargon: what to call it,
      and what it should do. What they write appears in Claude or ChatGPT beside ours the
      moment it saves.

      Shown only once something is connected — a box for writing prompts, to somebody who
      has no connector to show them in, is a control without a job (§6). -#}
  <section class="panel" id="prompts" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.your-own-asks", "Your own asks") }}</h2>
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.prompts-says", "Things you ask for often. Write one here and it appears in Claude or ChatGPT by name, next to ours.") }}</p>
    <ul class="series" id="prompt-rows"></ul>
    <label class="field">
      <span>{{ t("you.page.prompt-name", "What to call it") }}</span>
      <input type="text" id="prompt-name" maxlength="40" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off">
    </label>
    <label class="field">
      <span>{{ t("you.page.prompt-says", "What it should do") }}</span>
      <input type="text" id="prompt-says" maxlength="2000">
    </label>
    <p class="ending-row">
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet" id="prompt-save">{{ t("you.page.prompt-save", "Save it") }}</button>
    </p>
    <p class="said" id="prompts-said" hidden></p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="subscriptions" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.your-subscriptions", "Your subscriptions") }}</h2>
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.series-that-come-out-on-their-own", "Series that come out on their own clock. Follow one and we'll put each
      new instalment on Learn when it lands and email you.") }}</p>
    <ul class="series" id="series"></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" id="ending" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("you.page.your-account", "Your account") }}</h2>
    <div class="row">
      <a class="go-quiet" id="you-export" href="/account/export">{{ t("you.page.download-your-words", "Download your words") }}</a>
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet" id="you-out">{{ t("you.page.sign-out", "Sign out") }}</button>
    </div>
    {#- On a row of its own, in clay (2026-09-14): it stood beside Download and Sign out in
        the same quiet pill, three presses that looked alike and were nothing alike. -#}
    <div class="row ending-row">
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet danger" id="you-forget">{{ t("you.page.delete-account", "Delete account") }}</button>
      <button type="button" class="go-quiet" id="you-keep" hidden>{{ t("you.page.keep-my-account", "Keep my account") }}</button>
    </div>
    {#- Next to the button it describes, which is the only place anybody looks for it.
        The sentence stands without the link while the documents are shut: seven days is
        the fact somebody about to press this needs, and it is true either way. -#}
    <p class="note">{{ t("you.page.we-wait-seven-days-before-deleting", "We wait seven days before deleting.") }}
      {%- if legal_is_public() %}
      <a href="/deletion">{{ t("you.page.what-that-means", "What that means") }}</a>.
      {%- endif %}</p>
    <p class="said" id="you-ending-said" hidden></p>
  </section>
</main>
{% include '_foot.html.j2' %}

<script>
window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";
{#- The language menu stands in the bar here too, so the bar does not move between pages
    (2026-09-14). -#}
window.TARGUM_LANGUAGES = { {% for code, name in languages %}"{{ code }}": "{{ name }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} };
window.TARGUM_READING = {{ reading|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_INTO = {{ into|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_REQUIRED = {{ required|tojson }};
{% if strings %}window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};
{% endif %}</script>
<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('lang.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('account.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('you.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
